include includes/folio-shared.pug

html(lang="en")
    head
        +folioHead('The Design Dash: from a fuzzy problem to a connected plan | Aaron Benjamin', 'How I designed an open process that brings evidence, Object Guides, wireframes, and technical requirements into one connected product plan.', '/design-dash.html', 'folio', '/assets/img/folio/project--design-dash/dash-example-hero.png', 'dash-layout-20261004')
    body.folio.folio--case.folio--dash
        +folioShell
        +folioHeader('work', 'Design Dash', '/#design-dash')
        main#main.folio-main.folio-main--case
            article.folio-case
                +caseHero
                    .folio-case__intro
                        p.folio-eyebrow The Design Dash · Open-source design process
                        h1 From a fuzzy problem to a plan people can use.
                        p.folio-case__dek I designed The Design Dash to make a thorough product-design process more broadly available. It guides people through evidence, object modeling, and design decisions, then brings the work together in a plan with wireframes, technical requirements, and a way to validate what comes next.
                    figure.folio-case__hero-media
                        img(src="/assets/img/folio/project--design-dash/dash-example-hero.png" srcset=folioSrcset("/assets/img/folio/project--design-dash/dash-example-hero.png") sizes="(max-width: 600px) calc(100vw - 64px), (max-width: 900px) calc(100vw - 112px), 1100px" alt="Illustrative neighborhood tool library: tools on a shelf, a member borrowing a drill, and a simple borrowing interface" width="1672" height="941" fetchpriority="high")
                    +caseFacts
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Role
                            dd Process designer and author
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Purpose
                            dd Make rigorous product planning easier to access
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Foundation
                            dd OOUX, Object Guides, and a shared Object Library
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Output
                            dd A connected plan for design, implementation, and validation

                +caseSection({labelledby: "dash-files-title"})
                    p.folio-eyebrow I — Inspiration
                    h2#dash-files-title A finished screen can hide an unfinished idea
                    p Generating an interface is fast. Establishing what it should do takes evidence, a clear product model, and considered decisions. When that reasoning stays in scattered notes and conversations, a polished screen can conceal unresolved assumptions.
                    p I wanted The Design Dash to make that work approachable without reducing it to a prompt that produces screens. The intent is to help someone understand the problem, explore alternatives, and leave with a plan another person can inspect and use.
                    h3 One simple example throughout
                    p Imagine a neighborhood library that lends tools. Members need to find a tool, request to borrow it, and return it. This fictional Dash illustrates the method; its guides, wireframes, and rules are examples rather than findings from a real project.

                +caseSection({labelledby: "dash-rigor-title", tint: true, prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        p.folio-eyebrow D — Design Process
                        h2#dash-rigor-title Start with the things people understand
                        p Object-oriented UX (OOUX) provides the modeling foundation. Its ORCA process defines Objects, Relationships, Calls-to-action, and Attributes before turning them into navigation and screens. The tool-library example gives each object its own responsibility:
                        ul
                            li Tool records the item’s name and condition.
                            li Member identifies the person borrowing it.
                            li Loan connects the member and tool, records the due date, and moves from requested to checked out to returned.
                        p Availability depends on the Loan, rather than being a label added independently to every screen.
                        p Object Guides capture those definitions, including attributes, actions, relationships, lifecycle, business rules, and interface representations. They live in an Object Library, where future Dashes can reference them instead of rediscovering the same product concepts.
                    +caseFigure({id: "dash-object-guides", src: "/assets/img/folio/project--design-dash/dash-object-guides.png", alt: "Illustrative Object Guide excerpts for Tool, Member, and Loan, with their attributes, actions, relationship, and the Loan lifecycle", width: "1536", height: "1024", loading: "lazy", caption: "Fictional tool-library Dash: three Object Guides give the product a shared vocabulary."})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        h3 Reuse without silently changing the past
                        p A Dash references a shared guide until it needs a different definition. It can then fork that guide locally and promote reviewed learning back into the library. This adds some record-keeping, but protects earlier work while allowing the library to grow.

                +caseSection({labelledby: "dash-engagement", prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        p.folio-eyebrow E — Engagement
                        h2#dash-engagement Guide the work without requiring one tool
                        p A Dash begins with a problem, the people affected, the available evidence, and known constraints. Nine phases guide the work from opportunity and object modeling through flows, competing concepts, wireframes, and validation planning. Missing information stays visible as an assumption with a path to check it.
                        p The same process can run in a general AI chat, a file-capable agent, or a facilitated workshop. The method defines the work and its review gates; adapters handle how each environment records it. A coded prototype is optional, so access to a development environment does not determine whether someone can create a useful plan.
                        h3 Match the review to the consequences
                        p Risk, reversibility, and reach determine the review tier. A small, reversible change needs less process than a consequential workflow. Every tier retains an ethics floor; higher-risk work keeps its required gates. AI critique can help uncover questions, but cannot replace accountable human review.
                    +storyComparison({id: "dash-tiers-caption", title: "Three review tiers", columns: [{label: "Express", title: "Small and reversible", detail: "Keep the ethics floor. Track deferred evidence work."}, {label: "Standard", title: "A real workflow", detail: "Review evidence, alternatives, user understanding, ethics, and learning."}, {label: "High-stakes", title: "Greater consequences", detail: "Keep every required gate, with privacy and compliance review where applicable."}], caption: "The work determines the tier. The process records what remains unresolved."})

                +caseSection({labelledby: "dash-store-title", tint: true, prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        p.folio-eyebrow A — Action
                        h2#dash-store-title Carry the same decision through every artifact
                        p In the example Dash, one availability decision connects the plan:
                        ol
                            li Requirement: show whether a tool can be borrowed.
                            li Object rule: allow one checked-out Loan per Tool.
                            li Wireframe: distinguish available and on-loan tools.
                            li Technical check: confirm availability again at checkout.
                            li Research: test whether members understand those states.
                    +caseFigure({id: "dash-screen-record", src: "/assets/img/folio/project--design-dash/dash-connected-plan.png", alt: "Illustrative tool-library plan connecting an availability requirement, a Tool and Loan rule, borrowing wireframes, and technical checks, alongside evidence, research, and pitch artifacts", width: "1536", height: "1024", loading: "lazy", caption: "Fictional Dash artifacts: a requirement, an Object Guide, a wireframe, and technical notes describe the same behavior."})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        h3 A complete plan, with room for unanswered questions
                        p The Dash brings together:
                        ul
                            li Evidence, assumptions, opportunity framing, and success criteria.
                            li Object Guides, relationships, scenario flows, and page architecture.
                            li Concept comparisons, decision rationale, annotated wireframes, and edge states.
                            li Functional and technical requirements, accessibility considerations, a research plan, and a stakeholder pitch.
                        h3 One editable model; several useful views
                        p I chose linked Markdown records as the Dash Model rather than making a rich document or database compulsory. The current Design Plan and release Story are generated views of those records. That keeps the work portable and reduces duplicate editing, with a tradeoff: links and required information need validation to prevent drift.

                +caseOutcome("dash-outcome-title", "Make the process useful beyond the person who ran it", "S — Status and next steps")
                    p The intended benefit is a shared reference: designers can follow a screen back to a decision, developers can find its behavior and constraints, and reviewers can see what is supported by evidence and what still needs testing. The Object Library preserves understanding for the next Dash.
                    p I’m making The Design Dash open source so people can adapt the process to their own products and working environments. Its usefulness depends on the quality of the evidence and review people bring to it. A complete plan makes the next step clearer; it does not turn assumptions into validated results.
                    p My design goal is to make the reasoning accessible along with the artifacts—so a team can decide what to build, why it matters, and how to learn whether it worked.
                    p
                        a(href="https://github.com/abenjamin765/design-dash" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer") Explore The Design Dash on GitHub
            +caseStudyFooter('I’m happy to walk through how The Design Dash fits a product problem.', folioProjects[folioProjects['design-dash'].next])
